\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}
\usepackage{graphicx}

\title{Blade Passage Vibration Measurements on the K4 Rig}
\author{Turbomachinery Laboratory}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Rig Description}
The K4 rig is a single-stage axial turbine driven by
pressurised air from the laboratory main. For the present
campaign the stage was instrumented with twelve strain
gauges distributed over three blades, and tip timing was
recorded on every blade with the eight-probe array.

\section{Instrumentation}
Strain signals were conditioned in the rotating frame and
transmitted over the telemetry link at 200 kHz per channel.
The tip-timing probes were clocked against the once-per-rev
key phasor, and the trigger jitter was held below 40 ns for
the duration of the run.

\section{Results}
The strain map for the design point is shown in Figure
\ref{fig:blade}. The response is dominated by the first
flap mode over the whole speed range, with a secondary
response near 80\% speed that we attribute to the third
engine-order excitation crossing the second torsion mode.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\IfFileExists{blade_map.pdf}{\includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth,trim=1 2 3 4]{blade_map.pdf}}{\fbox{strain map pending}}
\caption{Strain amplitude over the blade surface at the
design point.}
\label{fig:blade}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\IfFileExists{campbell.pdf}{\includegraphics[width=0.5\textwidth]{campbell.pdf}}{\fbox{diagram pending}}
\caption{Campbell diagram for the instrumented blades.}
\label{fig:campbell}
\end{figure}

The crossing identified in the Campbell diagram coincides
with the secondary response to within the resolution of the
speed sweep, which supports the engine-order attribution.
Repeat runs at reduced inlet pressure reproduce the same
features, so the response is not an artefact of the drive
line.

\section{Uncertainty}
The strain gauges were calibrated against a shunt resistor
before each run, and the calibration drifted by less than
one percent across the campaign. Tip-timing clearance was
measured cold and corrected for casing growth using the
manufacturer's thermal model; the corrected clearances agree
with the borescope survey to within the probe tolerance.
\end{document}

